# 4. HTTP Packet Analysis

HTTP is an unencrypted application-layer protocol.

### Wireshark Filter

```text
http
```

Look for:

* GET
* POST
* HTTP response
* Host
* URI
* User-Agent
* Source IP
* Destination IP
* Source Port
* Destination Port
* Packet Length

Typical HTTP communication:

```text
Client
   |
   | HTTP GET
   v
Web Server
   |
   | HTTP Response
   v
Client
```

### Default Port

```text
TCP 80
```

### SOC Perspective

Because HTTP is unencrypted, packet capture may expose:

* URLs
* HTTP headers
* User-Agent
* Data transferred
* Potential credentials

---

# 5. HTTPS / TLS Packet Analysis

HTTPS encrypts HTTP communication using TLS.

### Wireshark Filters

```text
tls
```

or:

```text
tcp.port == 443
```

Analyze:

* Source IP
* Destination IP
* TCP Port
* TLS version
* TLS handshake
* Certificate information
* Packet size
* Connection timing

### Default Port

```text
TCP 443
```

Unlike HTTP, the actual application data is encrypted.

### SOC Perspective

Even when traffic is encrypted, analysts can investigate:

* Destination IP
* Domain information
* TLS metadata
* Certificate information
* Connection timing
* Traffic volume
* TLS fingerprints such as JA3/JA4, when available

